    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "This study validates the critical role of ionizable lipids with a pKa of 6.0–7.0 in driving rapid endocytic entry followed by pH-dependent membrane destabilization via protonation-induced fusion of the endosomal vesicle with the lysosomal membrane. By optimizing the lipid-to-lipid and lipid-to-nucleic acid electrostatic interactions, researchers ensure efficient dissociation of the mRNA payload from the lipid bilayer into the cytosol, bypassing degradative pathways within the endolysosome. Concurrently, the strategic incorporation of PEGylated zwitterionic lipids suppresses non-specific protein adsorption (opsonization) and micellar re-aggregation, thereby minimizing rapid clearance by the reticuloendothelial system (RES) while maintaining a narrow polydisperse particle size distribution. Ultimately, this precise supramolecular engineering of the nucleic acid-lipid complex maximizes translational efficiency and therapeutic index, establishing a robust molecular framework for advanced surface-delivery systems in mRNA gene therapy.",
      "source": "target",
